news i'm lana nguyen i'm a finance  reporter here  in new york and i am talking to killer  mike  aka michael render nice to me nice to  have you here michael thank you  hey luanne how you doing i'm great thank  you so  today we are talking about black owned  banks mike and i have been talking about  this for a couple of months now  and uh he's finally found time in his  busy schedule promoting his album and  promoting his new venture greenwood um  to talk to us  and so i just wanted to open the  conversation mike to discuss  hashtag bank black what is this movement  and why did you start supporting it  several years ago well let me let me  first establish i've banked black my  whole life i didn't have a choice  betty and willie who were my  grandparents uh marched me and my  sisters down to  a black bank when we were children and  we all got accounts and  they had done that as young people and  their parents had also done that  um so i'm from a tradition over a  hundred year tradition  of banking black and um i want to tell  people that bank black is not  exclusionary and greenwood is not  exclusionary  it is created to circumvent some  problems that  worker-class people no matter what color  have big banks are pulling out of  working class in  in poor communities to be frank many of  those communities  disenfranchised housed black and brown  people  in their place came predatory lenders i  mean predatory  um people who cash checks like check  cash in places liquor sorts things that  nature  where they were being charged 20 25  cents on a dollar cash check  this gives them a card that is  essentially  not taking money from them every time  they use it it gives them the ability to  bank  fully in there um to do their banking  fully from their phones which gives them  a freedom  that the brick and mortar banks having  left and robbed them up  so the back the bank black movement is  the first step in the american dream and  the  first step is to be able to do simple  things like buy a home and a car  make sure you have a savings make sure  you have something to save for  retirement  it helps working-class people work their  way and matriculate their way up the  ladder  it helps working-class people do things  like buy a first home  to make sure that they have something to  leave their children and they have  something  that's um that is accrued that it's  worth something that that establishes  well  um everybody's not going to be a  millionaire a zillionaire billionaire  but that does not mean that people  cannot have dignity and being a part of  the working class and middle class in  this country  and banking black allows black people a  disenfranchised community  that had been denied through terrible  acts such as slavery jim crowism  redlining and now brick and mortar banks  being pulled out  um they have just been terribly treated  and i think that this does a lot  to restore the ability to achieve the  american dream to have some sense of  equity and equality  and to not only service the black  community but when the black community  is stronger financially  the greater community is stronger  financially because in capitalism  competition ends up helping the consumer  more than anyone else  great and you know we've talked about  black owned banks extensively and you're  always shouting out different firms you  even did that when we were preparing for  this call just now right so  you talk about black owned banks a lot  now that you're kind of coming into the  scene with your own digital bank  um how does that affect you know what  you say about this movement now your  competitor you know how has that been  received  well there's no shortage of churches  liquor stores and barber shops in my  community  so you could never get enough haircuts  jesus or whiskey  and i think that i think that banking  can work the same way  i think that we are new that we are um  of course you want to be the biggest  baddest business on the block and i  think we're going to grow to be there  with that said i believe that um most  working class people  so if you're poor it might be a little  harder you've got one bank account then  you're saving under the mattress like i  still do  and i get that but if you have an extra  bank account to save toward christmas  if you have a bank account to set up for  your babies i encourage you to set it up  at any black bank whether it's carver  whether it's one united whether citizens  trust  whether it's any of those banks i  encourage you to do it because we need  to be taking care of our own community  without the bank of italy that was  started by italian immigrants who were  coming through  from ellis island to make sure they had  the ability to lend to  start businesses we would never have  bank of america which it grew into  so if we don't support black banks we're  not going to find it easy to find  support in the banking space  but i'd like to add that there's a new  dog on the block man and she's walking  around  trying to be seen and that is greenwood  we have the ability  to create a lot of noise to focus that  black dollar and the ability to  facilitate  fairness in the banking institution so  we want to add to the legacy of black  banks not necessarily take away  i don't want to see another black bank  disappear what i'd like to see is  cooperation collaboration and a bigger  conglomeration  and i think that time will tell them  we'll see what happens and we were  talking about the partnership that you  are  right now negotiating with you know a  partner bank  that can't be named yet but i want to  ask you about  these big main street banks if you do  end up partnering with a big  firm how do you make sure that they  ensure the mission of  greenwood is carried through that racial  justice and equality  are something that's going to be upheld  by that bank as opposed to maybe  perpetuated  um you know or inequality being  perpetuated i like to go back to a story  where  wells fargo as we know their their uh  their ceo just got into a little trouble  saying he couldn't find black talent um  i'd like to push back and challenge to  say that i believe black talon's out  there and if it's not  what are we going to do to be like a  sports franchise and develop the black  talent so  where he sees a lack of black talent i'd  like to encourage him to partner with at  least three schools in the metropolitan  atlanta area start with kindergarten  um and mentor those kids through 13  years of school and get those kids into  college and graduate without what you  want to see  much like baseball invest in the  caribbean to grow talent much like the  nba  investing in the cities in uh united  states i think that you should be  investing in talent and i think there's  talent out there  beyond that though i think that there is  a real will and want to learn what is  the right thing to do in this moment  and there is a sincere effort to partner  and have true collaborative partnerships  where you're listening to the partner  who may not have the biggest person who  may have the  most experience in this direct contact  with that community so i think that  right now corporations want to learn  because they not only realize  that it's wrong morally or wrong  philosophically  i think that they understand it's bad  for business to have black people out of  the financial loop  in this country so i think that whatever  partner we choose to go with  is going to become a better corporation  for having partnered to us and it's  going to give us a wider  spread you know i think if we're all  doing a little bit no one will have to  do a lot  but i think a lot needs to be done to  make up for a lot of the things that  were done in the past  and mike um so in episode one of trigger  warning you tried to spend all your  money at black owned businesses  and you really struggled right so my  question for you is how do you avoid  that  now that you're opening a bank that you  know is aimed towards  black and and latinx people how do you  make sure that you aren't you know kind  of being too narrow in your focus  well we're not because you know the  beautiful thing about this country is  it's for everyone  you know i i i do business with every  type of nationality person  whether it's barbering or the restaurant  business or real estate business  i'm a few different businesses and i see  all types of people  i encourage people whether it's in my  music or my daily talks  to make sure that you have a friend an  ally or someone in your life that's not  from your racial ethnic background  that is not a bisexual persuasion does  not if your own spiritual belief because  honestly  that's the only thing that makes  collaboration of cooperation work so  effectively in this country when it does  so  i believe that in the same way you know  i believe there are three  three ways i define black businesses  you're owned by black people  totally ran by black people or you're  owned by people other than black but to  employ black people which is probably  for the community or if you're a small  business that cannot  be um higher or being owned by black  people that you do  my community fairly i think that we have  the ability to do that in terms of  cooperation and collaboration  and i think that we're going to do it  okay and then just a final question and  again this is a big picture question  how has the banking industry contributed  to inequality  racial inequality in the us and what  does greenwood want to do about that  redlining we know was um we know that it  was damn near possible in cities like  chicago  for black people to get some of the sun  the same loans that they deserve  we know the gi bill um was not honored  when black soldiers came home from world  war  ii we know that where you have  comparable um  comparable people applying for loans  that white people get turned down an  eight percent rate black people are  turned out at a 21  rate i know that their businesses with  nuances that people don't understand  like  when you're talking about getting a  small business loan for a barber shop it  could be much more hard at a larger bank  because they don't even understand black  guys go get their hair cut  four times a month they have to go every  week 25 a week is 100  a month you know that's about 1200 a  year they don't get that concept because  their customers go to get a haircut  every three months you understand so  there's there's a need for for this  there i think that there  is a tradition of not helping the black  community  um in all institutions not just banking  institutions  we've been failed by the medical  institutions we've been failed by the  political machines  um we've been failed by virtually at the  education institution  but i think that banking can understand  even faster than some of the other  institutions  that the black dollar is a powerful  force in this economy  and if we help the black community get  financially literate and properly focus  at black dollar to grow business  to further help competition in this  country that the  banking systems wins greater society  wins the black community wins  and overall the customer wins okay mike  i gotta ask one question for the fans uh  you're you're  parlaying your business skills into  finance right now but uh what does that  mean for your music career  i'm gonna keep singing and dance until  the day i die i love my first job and my  wife  who's a far more brilliant businessman  than that reminds me that ultimately  michael i'll handle the numbers you just  keep singing and dancing  great okay thank you very much killer  mike michael render  appreciate your time today i appreciate  you a lot of respect have a great day

